    day: 11, title: "Babel scattered",
    month: "January", month_index: 1, season: "ordinary", season_label: "Ordinary Time",
    theme: "God of Beginnings & Sovereign Purpose", doctrine: "Divine Sovereignty",
    scripture: "Genesis 11:1-9 · Proverbs 19:21 · Acts 2:1-12 · Revelation 7:9",
    scripture_block: "Now the whole earth had one language and the same words. And as people migrated from the east, they found a plain in the land of Shinar and settled there. And they said to one another, \"Come, let us make bricks, and burn them thoroughly.\" And they said, \"Come, let us build ourselves a city and a tower with its top in the heavens, and let us make a name for ourselves, lest we be dispersed over the face of the whole earth.\"",
    scripture_ref: "Genesis 11:1-4",
    excerpt: "Babel was not about architecture. It was about autonomy. Humanity united — not to worship God, but to replace Him. God's scattering of the nations was an act of mercy hiding inside an act of judgment.",
    life_lesson: "Unity is not an absolute good. Unified rebellion is worse than scattered humility. Examine your ambitions today: are you building for God's name or your own?",
    content: `<p class="post-dropcap">After the flood, God commanded humanity to "be fruitful and multiply and fill the earth" (<span class="scripture-ref" data-full="And you, be fruitful and multiply, increase greatly on the earth and multiply in it.">Genesis 9:7</span>). At Babel, humanity decided they had a better idea: stay together, build a city, construct a tower, and make a name for themselves. "Lest we be dispersed" is the operative phrase — a direct defiance of God's command. The sin of Babel is not ambition. It is autonomous ambition — ambition that excludes God and exalts self. The tower was not designed to reach heaven literally. It was designed to reach significance apart from the Creator. "Let us make a name for ourselves" is the creed of every fallen human civilization.</p>

<p>God's response is both judgment and mercy. He confuses their language and scatters them. The judgment is obvious: the project fails. The mercy is less obvious but more important: God prevents humanity from uniting in full-scale rebellion. Proverbs 19:21 states the principle: "Many are the plans in the mind of a man, but it is the purpose of the LORD that will stand." Human unity without God is a weapon. Babel teaches that God will break what must be broken to save what must be saved. A scattered humanity is better than a unified humanity marching toward destruction. The scattering at Babel was a wound that prevented a death.</p>

<p>Pentecost is the reversal of Babel. At Babel, God confused languages to scatter the nations. At Pentecost, the apostles spoke languages they had never learned, and people from every nation heard the gospel in their own tongue (<span class="scripture-ref" data-full="And they were amazed and astonished, saying, 'Are not all these who are speaking Galileans? And how is it that we hear, each of us in his own native language?'">Acts 2:7-8</span>). God scattered the nations to prevent unified rebellion. God gathered the nations to extend unified salvation. Revelation 7:9 completes the arc: "a great multitude that no one could number, from every nation, from all tribes and peoples and languages, standing before the throne." The scattering was provisional. The gathering is eternal.</p>`
